    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\nAn interface defines the expected shape of an object and is useful for public contracts and extensible object designs.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nRead the type from left to right, identify what is checked at compile time, then trace one small runtime example.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\ninterface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nfunction greet(\n  user: User,\n): string {\n  return `Hello ${user.name}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet({\n  id: \"1\",\n  name: \"Ada\",\n}));\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / IDIOMATIC TypeScript:\n```ts\ninterface User {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n}\n\nconst user = {\n  id: \"1\",\n  name: \"Ada\",\n} satisfies User;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Example result:\n```text\nHello Ada\n```\n\nStep 6 — Complexity / trade-off.\nUse interfaces for stable object contracts and extension points.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases.\nCheck null/undefined, unions, widening/narrowing, inference loss, runtime boundaries, incorrect assertions, and API evolution where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway.\nExplain what the compiler proves, what exists at runtime, when the idiomatic/built-in approach is preferable, and the maintainability trade-off.",
